            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="3" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="3" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house is reasonably air tight, with a few annoying air leakage areas that wel identified together during the blower door test. These current air leakage locations include:



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** The basement windows have leakage and may need replaced &amp; both entry doors could use an upgrade to the perimeter weather-stripping &amp; door sweep (just routine maintenance)



** An intentional hole is the co-vented chimney for the current boiler and hot water tank (that can be eliminated with a combination heating/hot water boiler upgrade)



** An intentional hole is the 5" combustion air supply pipe for the boiler &amp; hot water tank - that can also be eliminated with a combination heating/hot water boiler upgrade



Savings: By addressing the above-mentioned air leakage locations, you could potentially save $ 69 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.8 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for reducing air leakage.



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <foundation>

Your basement has concrete block walls and about 91% of the perimeter has been finished on the interior surface with an insulated stud wall and a drywall finished surface. The other 9% (12' of wall in the laundry room) is still bare block wall. About 40% of the basement walls are above grade and fully exposed to outside temperatures.



I recall you saying there was a flood down there and you recently had the whole basement upgraded. You might think that having 91% of the basement walls insulated is adequate, however the other 9% (the laundry room) will suck a lot of heat out of your house (especially the 40% of the wall above grade). Using my Infrared Camera on the outside of the house, I could clearly see the heat coming through that section of foundation wall.



For comparison, you could have 1 bad window or one small/medium size hole to the outside, and that one location will become an energy drain on the whole house. This is what is happening in your laundry room area.



I have modeled insulating the laundry room wall with the same 2x4 (R12) wall oer just having it covered with R10 of rigid foam insulation (and covered with a drywall surface).



Savings: By insulating the remaining 9% of basement walls, you could potentially save $ 932 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 38.1 GJ. As the wall area to be insulated is less than our minimum 20%, there are no rebates for this upgrade ......... however the energy savings should benefit you greatly.



</foundation>
                <windows>

The 4 basement windows are older &amp; very drafty. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading the basement windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 32 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.3 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</windows>
                <doors>

DOORS



Both of the exterior doors in this house are steel insulated doors with double pane glass windows. The doors are generally in good shape, but I recommend you replace the weather stripping around the interior of the frame to reduce draft, and increase comfort. This is especially important in the master bedroom.



Rebates: There are currently no rebates available for installing new doors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 151 litre gas fired hot water tank, that co-vents together with the boiler up a chimney . We discussed an option for you to consider replacing both the older boiler and this hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency (95% AFUE) condensing propane "Combination" boiler that can both heat your house and provide instantaneous on-demand hot water.



Please see the previous Heating System upgrade recommendation for further information.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 82.2% efficient gas boiler, and there is also a gas hot water tank in the same mechanical closet. They both co-vent up the same chimney, and there is a 5" combustion air supply pipe located in that room to provide combustion air to these 2 appliances. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency (95% AFUE) condensing propane "Combination" boiler that can both heat your house and provide instantaneous on-demand hot water. This will eliminate the need for the current chimney &amp; the combustion air pipe .......... and will create a lot more room inside that mechanical closet.



Savings: By replacing your older boiler and hot water tank with a 95% AFUE condensing gas combination boiler, you could potentially save $ 1,219 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 48.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). While updating your boiler, you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for up to $175 in rebates per pump (up to 2 per fiscal year) from EEIP.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

VENTILATION



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - A kitchen range hood fan

        - 1 bathroom fan

        - A clothes dryer



I would recommend you replace the bathroom fan on the main floor with a new ENERGY STAR?????? rated unit. You may want to consider installing a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat. As your house could use a little more ventilation, you could even install an automatic timer switch to turn the main floor bathroom fan on/off a few times per day for 20-30 minutes.



As outlined in the previous Air Sealing section of this report, your house is relatively air tight and is expected to become tighter if you upgrade the boiler &amp; hot water (eliminates the chimney &amp; combustion air pipes) and replace those 4 basement windows. If your house was being built today, it would have a "full house" Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed.



Almost all houses from the 60's &amp; 70's would not have had an HRV and instead would manage fresh air supply &amp; indoor air quality by using exhaust fans (kitchen, bathroom, clothes dryer) and incorporate a 4" or 5" fresh air supply pipe connected to the return air plenum of the furnace - that would allow outside air each time the furnace operates or when an exhaust fan is operating. Your house does not have a furnace (&amp; therefore no air circulation ductwork), so it mostly relies on exhaust fans to increase airflow through the house.



Building science tells us to "Renovate Tight" and also "Ventilate Right". I think you can achieve adequate ventilation if you replace the main floor bathroom fan with a new ENERGY STAR?????? rated unit (outlined above) and incorporate a timer switch to either automatically or manually increase the amount of ventilation.



If you ever notice excess condensation building up on the main floor windows, you may need to increase the amount of ventilation (run the bathroom fan more).



Savings: The previous air sealing section of the report mentioned about $69 of estimated savings. The additional "as required" ventilation outlined above is estimated to cost you about $15 per year.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Fort Smith, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood, the bathroom fan and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 4.4 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current boiler &amp; hot water tank vent up a chimney &amp; can be susceptible to back drafting of carbon monoxide into the house ......... however this risk would be completely eliminated if you complete the upgrade to a new high efficiency combination boiler &amp; hot water heater (the chimney will be eliminated).



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $0.80 per litre 

Propane - $2.46 per m3 for the piped system; or $0.66 per litre delivered 

Wood - $315.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.20 per bag, $268.12 per tonne bulk delivered



